<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/foundational/carbon-cycle-climate">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Carbon is the skeleton of life and the currency of climate. It cycles through four great
    reservoirs — <strong>atmosphere</strong> (~880 GtC), <strong>oceans</strong> (~38,000 GtC),
    <strong>soils</strong> (~1,600 GtC) and <strong>living vegetation</strong> (~550 GtC) —
    moved by photosynthesis (~120 GtC/yr), respiration, weathering and volcanism. The cycle ran
    in balance for millennia. Since about 1850, humans have been adding a bonus line to the
    ledger: burning fossil carbon that took hundreds of millions of years to bury, at a rate of
    roughly <strong>10 GtC per year</strong> (≈37 Gt of CO₂).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Keeling curve — direct CO₂ measurement at Mauna Loa since 1958; the sharpest turn in the record" widget="plotter" config={keelingCfg} />

  <p>
    Charles Keeling started measuring in 1958 at 315 parts per million (ppm). The line has risen
    every year since and now passes 420 ppm — the highest in at least 800,000 years of ice-core
    record. Each ppm is 2.13 billion tonnes of carbon, and about half of what we emit is absorbed
    by oceans and land; the rest accumulates here.
  </p>

  <h2>Why CO₂ moves the thermostat</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The greenhouse mechanism:</strong> sunlight passes through CO₂ easily; the infrared Earth re-radiates gets absorbed and re-emitted back down. More CO₂, thicker blanket.</li>
    <li><strong>The measured result:</strong> global average surface temperature is roughly 1.2–1.5 °C above the late-1800s baseline, with 2024 the hottest calendar year recorded.</li>
    <li><strong>The physics is old news:</strong> Eunice Foote (1856) and John Tyndall (1859) demonstrated CO₂'s heat-trapping in the laboratory before cars existed.</li>
    <li><strong>The policy marker:</strong> the 2015 Paris Agreement set holding warming "well below 2 °C", aiming for 1.5 °C — the curve above is what those numbers fight against.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Watch the interactions: warmer oceans absorb <em>less</em> CO₂ and hold less oxygen;
    thawing permafrost releases more methane. Feedbacks, not linearity, are why the problem
    deserves the whole field of environmental science.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NOAA Global Monitoring Laboratory (Mauna Loa data); IPCC AR6 (2021); Global Carbon Budget.</p>
</TopicPage>
